1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:16,738
Last time, a blind father in a tent said, the voice is the voice of Jacob, but the
hands are the hands of Esau. And he chose the hands.

2
00:00:16,788 --> 00:00:27,830
Now go more than fifty years on. The son who stood in the goatskin is a hundred and
thirty years old, and he is standing outside his own tent, near Hebron.

3
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:41,389
Imagine it. His sons have just come home from Egypt, and they are all talking at
once, about a brother he buried in his heart twenty years ago. Joseph is alive.

4
00:00:41,439 --> 00:00:50,820
Joseph rules Egypt. He does not believe a word of it. And then he stops listening to
them, because of a sound coming up the road.

5
00:00:54,585 --> 00:01:00,080
[Israel] Words. You can bring me words all day. Those are wagons.

6
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:04,897
That is imagined.

7
00:01:04,947 --> 00:01:17,162
What Genesis says is that he was stunned, because he did not believe them, and that
when he saw the wagons Joseph had sent to carry him back, his spirit revived.

8
00:01:17,212 --> 00:01:26,100
Here is Brian, or his words at least, by way of Mister Scaredy Cat, on what a blind
man hears in that.

9
00:01:26,927 --> 00:01:33,706
[Brian] Provision. That is what I hear coming up that road. Not just proof that
Joseph was alive, though it was that too.

10
00:01:33,756 --> 00:01:40,033
[Brian] Wagons, and grain, and a way to get an old man and seventy people to food in
a famine.

11
00:01:40,083 --> 00:01:51,670
[Brian] God told Jacob at Bethel, I am with you and will not leave you, and then He
kept doing exactly that: at Laban's house, on the road home, at the meeting with
Esau,

12
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:53,465
[Brian] at the wagons, in Goshen.

13
00:01:53,515 --> 00:02:00,062
[Brian] Jacob could not see most of it coming, and he did not trust what he could
see. It came anyway.

14
00:02:00,112 --> 00:02:08,097
[Brian] That is where I sit as I read his life, moved and a little amazed at how God
provided for this man at every turn after He said He would.

15
00:02:08,147 --> 00:02:17,790
[Brian] And as a child of God, that speaks to my heart more than my eyes ever did.
It is a comfort no sight could give me, because I am not being asked to see it
coming.

16
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:21,370
[Brian] I am being asked to trust the One who is bringing it.

17
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:33,808
That afternoon was the last time Jacob needed to see something before he would
believe it. Seventeen years later, in a bed in Goshen, he cannot see.

18
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:35,170
And he will not need to.

19
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:49,298
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

20
00:02:49,348 --> 00:02:57,732
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

21
00:02:57,782 --> 00:03:00,650
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

22
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:12,611
This episode is part of our series on sight lost and not given back: six people in
the Bible who lost their sight and lived the rest of their lives without it.

23
00:03:12,661 --> 00:03:28,223
We tell them in three pairs. Two men blinded by their enemies, Zedekiah and Samson.
A father and a son, Isaac and Jacob. Two old men of Shiloh, Eli and Ahijah.

24
00:03:28,273 --> 00:03:33,906
Every episode begins with sounds, and with what a blind man hears in them.

25
00:03:33,956 --> 00:03:46,912
Along the way we follow the Hebrew words the Bible uses for eyes that fail, one word
handing on to the next. When we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

26
00:03:46,962 --> 00:03:54,280
Everything else is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice, and
from Brian.

27
00:03:54,830 --> 00:04:06,826
First, where we are, and it takes a whole life to say, because Jacob spent his whole
life learning one thing: that eyes cannot be trusted.

28
00:04:06,876 --> 00:04:09,164
He learned it first by using it.

29
00:04:09,214 --> 00:04:21,622
In his father's tent he put on goatskin and his brother's clothes and lied to a
blind man, and the blind man's hands and nose and mouth all told him the lie was
true.

30
00:04:21,672 --> 00:04:31,400
Jacob walked out with the blessing and learned that a man who cannot see can be
fooled by everything else. Then it was done to him.

31
00:04:31,450 --> 00:04:43,075
He ran to Haran and worked seven years for Rachel, and on the wedding night his
uncle put the wrong sister into his bed in the dark. When morning came, there was
Leah.

32
00:04:43,125 --> 00:04:53,572
The dark had done to Jacob what blindness had done to his father, and when he asked
why he had been deceived, Laban's answer was about the younger and the older.

33
00:04:53,622 --> 00:05:05,717
Jacob was being paid back in his own coin. Twenty years later, going home, he spent
a night alone by the river Jabbok wrestling a man in the night, until daybreak.

34
00:05:05,767 --> 00:05:12,177
Genesis never says what the man looked like, and when Jacob asked his name he did
not get it.

35
00:05:12,227 --> 00:05:22,278
Yet when it was over he named the place Peniel, the face of God, and said, I have
seen God face to face.

36
00:05:22,328 --> 00:05:35,016
A night, a stranger with no name, a question not answered, and Jacob called it
seeing. Seeing was already something other than eyes to him.

37
00:05:35,066 --> 00:05:44,680
Then his eyes lied to him outright. His sons brought him Joseph's robe dipped in the
blood of a goat, and said, examine it.

38
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:52,557
He looked at it, and he recognized it, and he said, Joseph has surely been torn to
pieces.

39
00:05:52,607 --> 00:06:01,283
His eyes were right about the robe and wrong about everything that mattered, and he
believed them for twenty years.

40
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:12,265
The man who had fooled his father with a garment and a goat was fooled by his sons
with a garment and a goat. And then the wagons, where we began.

41
00:06:12,315 --> 00:06:14,179
He was a hundred and thirty.

42
00:06:14,229 --> 00:06:25,348
His sons came back from Egypt and told him Joseph was alive and ruling the whole
land, and the text says he was stunned, because he did not believe them.

43
00:06:25,398 --> 00:06:33,847
True words, and he would not take them. What changed his mind was not what they
said. It was what he saw.

44
00:06:33,897 --> 00:06:46,523
When he saw the wagons Joseph had sent to carry him back, the spirit of their father
Jacob was revived, and he said, enough, my son Joseph is still alive,

45
00:06:46,573 --> 00:06:49,321
I will go to see him before I die.

46
00:06:49,371 --> 00:06:59,939
So at a hundred and thirty, after the tent, and the dark, and the river, and the
robe, he was still a man who had to see a thing before he would believe it.

47
00:06:59,989 --> 00:07:08,220
He went down to Egypt, and when he met Joseph he said, finally I can die, now that I
have seen your face.

48
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:15,855
He did not die. He lived seventeen more years in Goshen, and in those years his eyes
went.

49
00:07:15,905 --> 00:07:26,545
By the chapter we are reading he is a hundred and forty-seven, he has made Joseph
swear to carry him home to the family grave, and he is ill.

50
00:07:26,595 --> 00:07:37,971
Joseph is told, and he comes, and he brings his two sons. Keep the whole chain in
mind, because this chapter is the end of it. For once, Jacob cannot see.

51
00:07:38,021 --> 00:07:42,026
And for once, he does not need to. Where this is.

52
00:07:42,076 --> 00:07:51,241
Stand in Goshen, in the green north-east of Egypt, where the Nile spreads out toward
the sea, and face north, with north at twelve o'clock.

53
00:07:51,291 --> 00:08:03,774
Nothing from the rest of this series is near you. The land of the promise is a long
way off at two o'clock, and three places in it are named in this one chapter.

54
00:08:03,824 --> 00:08:13,726
Hebron, where Abraham and Isaac lie in the cave Jacob has just made Joseph swear to
carry him to, is about two weeks' walk at two o'clock.

55
00:08:13,776 --> 00:08:20,361
Bethlehem, where he buried Rachel beside the road, is a few hours beyond it on the
same line.

56
00:08:20,411 --> 00:08:30,682
And Bethel, which he still calls Luz, where God appeared to him on his way out with
nothing, is a day past that. Keep those three in mind.

57
00:08:30,732 --> 00:08:36,580
A blind man in Egypt is about to name every one of them from his bed.

58
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:43,510
Genesis chapter 48, verses 1 to 11.

59
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:57,068
[Reader] Some time later Joseph was told, “Your father is ill.” So he set out with
his two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim. When Jacob was told, “Your son Joseph has come
to you,”

60
00:08:57,118 --> 00:09:01,020
[Reader] Israel rallied his strength and sat up in bed.

61
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:09,221
[Reader] Jacob said to Joseph, “God Almighty appeared to me at Luz in the land of
Canaan, and there He blessed me and told me, ‘Behold,

62
00:09:09,271 --> 00:09:20,090
[Reader] I will make you fruitful and multiply you; I will make you a multitude of
peoples, and will give this land to your descendants after you as an everlasting
possession.’

63
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:31,570
Then he adopts his two grandsons as his own sons, as if they were Reuben and Simeon,
and his mind goes to the road. Verse 7.

64
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:43,860
[Reader] And now your two sons born to you in Egypt before I came to you here shall
be reckoned as mine; Ephraim and Manasseh shall be mine, just as Reuben and Simeon
are mine.

65
00:09:43,910 --> 00:09:56,114
[Reader] Any children born to you after them shall be yours, and they shall be
called by the names of their brothers in the territory they inherit.

66
00:09:56,164 --> 00:10:06,777
[Reader] Now as for me, when I was returning from Paddan, to my sorrow Rachel died
along the way in the land of Canaan, some distance from Ephrath.

67
00:10:06,827 --> 00:10:11,830
[Reader] So I buried her there beside the road to Ephrath” (that is, Bethlehem).

68
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:23,767
Hear what he did in those verses, because it is a legal act. Ephraim and Manasseh
shall be mine, like Reuben and Simeon.

69
00:10:23,817 --> 00:10:35,861
He adopted his two grandsons as his own sons, which is why there are tribes of
Ephraim and Manasseh in Israel and no tribe of Joseph, and why Joseph, through them,

70
00:10:35,911 --> 00:10:39,867
got two shares of the land where his brothers got one.

71
00:10:39,917 --> 00:10:47,274
Matthew Henry calls it a charter of adoption. And hear who those two were, because
they were not children.

72
00:10:47,324 --> 00:10:56,152
Joseph was thirty when he stood before Pharaoh, and his two sons were born in the
seven good years that followed, before the famine.

73
00:10:56,202 --> 00:11:02,948
Jacob came down in the second year of the famine, at a hundred and thirty, and lived
seventeen more.

74
00:11:02,998 --> 00:11:12,331
Count it through and Joseph is about fifty-six at this bedside, and Manasseh and
Ephraim are grown men, somewhere in their twenties.

75
00:11:12,381 --> 00:11:21,785
The Bible calls them lads in the blessing, the way an old man does. Grown men, then,
kneeling at an old man's knees.

76
00:11:21,835 --> 00:11:33,393
Joseph had been made an Egyptian to serve Pharaoh: an Egyptian name,
Zaphenath-paneah, and an Egyptian wife, Asenath, the daughter of the priest of On,

77
00:11:33,443 --> 00:11:35,238
the city of the sun god.

78
00:11:35,288 --> 00:11:45,383
Ephraim and Manasseh were half Egyptian, grandsons of a pagan priest, born in a
country whose people would not sit at a table with a Hebrew.

79
00:11:45,433 --> 00:11:56,955
Jacob names the one thing that could have kept them out, born to you in Egypt, and
overrides it with a word. Their mother's blood did not decide what they were.

80
00:11:57,005 --> 00:12:05,469
Their grandfather did. Why two shares for one son? Because it is the firstborn's
double portion.

81
00:12:05,519 --> 00:12:16,156
Reuben was the firstborn and lost it, Chronicles says, for defiling his father's
bed, and the birthright went to the sons of Joseph, the firstborn of Rachel,

82
00:12:16,206 --> 00:12:18,198
the wife Jacob had served for.

83
00:12:18,248 --> 00:12:28,098
The way you give one son a double share of land is to make his two sons two tribes.
Then Luz, and the road to Bethlehem.

84
00:12:28,148 --> 00:12:39,226
He has not seen either in seventeen years and will not see them again, and he places
them from his bed as exactly as a man who still walks them.

85
00:12:39,276 --> 00:12:45,470
And then, verse 8, having just named the two men and made them his, he asks.

86
00:12:49,230 --> 00:12:59,474
[Reader] When Israel saw the sons of Joseph, he asked, “Who are these?” Joseph said
to his father, “They are the sons God has given me in this place.”

87
00:12:59,524 --> 00:13:10,324
[Reader] So Jacob said, “Please bring them to me, that I may bless them.” Now
Israel’s eyesight was poor because of old age; he could hardly see.

88
00:13:10,374 --> 00:13:18,446
[Reader] Joseph brought his sons to him, and his father kissed them and embraced
them. “I never expected to see your face again,”

89
00:13:18,496 --> 00:13:23,340
[Reader] Israel said to Joseph, “but now God has let me see your children as well.”

90
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:34,846
Two verses, and they seem to disagree. Verse 8 says Israel saw the sons of Joseph.
Verse 10 says he could hardly see.

91
00:13:34,896 --> 00:13:42,437
Put them together and you have a man who can tell there are two shapes in the room
and cannot tell whose they are.

92
00:13:42,487 --> 00:13:51,141
He had named them both, Ephraim and Manasseh, a moment before. Now he cannot tell
which shape is which.

93
00:13:51,191 --> 00:13:57,215
Many blind people live exactly there, and Brian will say so in a moment.

94
00:13:57,265 --> 00:14:09,385
Some readers think the question was also part of the ceremony, a father formally
asking who stands before him before he blesses them, the way Isaac asked who are
you,

95
00:14:09,435 --> 00:14:15,177
and Joseph's answer does sound formal: the sons God has given me in this place.

96
00:14:15,227 --> 00:14:24,837
The text does not say that. What it does say, in the very next verse, is why he
asked. So he does what Isaac did.

97
00:14:24,887 --> 00:14:33,839
He asks who they are, and he has them brought close, and he kisses them and holds
them. The hands again.

98
00:14:33,889 --> 00:14:46,312
And then he says a thing that only a man in his condition would put that way: God
has let me see your children. He cannot make out their faces. He is holding them.

99
00:14:46,362 --> 00:14:48,430
That is what seeing means to him now.

100
00:14:49,438 --> 00:14:59,329
[Brian] Isaac asked who was there, and he was lied to. Jacob asked who was there,
and he was told the truth. Same question, same family, one generation apart.

101
00:14:59,379 --> 00:15:06,175
[Brian] Asking is not the hard part. Every blind person asks who is in the room. The
hard part is what comes after.

102
00:15:06,225 --> 00:15:13,723
[Brian] Somebody answers you, and you have to decide whether to believe them. Isaac
believed a lie and gave away the blessing.

103
00:15:13,773 --> 00:15:23,688
[Brian] Jacob believed the truth, and then went on to trust his own hands over his
son's eyes. Two blind men, two answers, two very different endings.

104
00:15:23,738 --> 00:15:25,820
[Brian] That is what this episode is about.

105
00:15:26,370 --> 00:15:28,240
Verses 12 to 20.

106
00:15:28,925 --> 00:15:34,898
[Reader] Then Joseph removed his sons from his father’s knees and bowed facedown.

107
00:15:34,948 --> 00:15:40,869
[Reader] And Joseph took both of them—with Ephraim in his right hand toward Israel’s
left hand,

108
00:15:40,919 --> 00:15:46,911
[Reader] and Manasseh in his left hand toward Israel’s right hand—and brought them
close to him.

109
00:15:46,961 --> 00:15:57,112
[Reader] But Israel stretched out his right hand and put it on the head of Ephraim,
the younger; and crossing his hands, he put his left on Manasseh’s head,

110
00:15:57,162 --> 00:15:59,610
[Reader] although Manasseh was the firstborn.

111
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:11,079
[Reader] Then he blessed Joseph and said: “May the God before whom my fathers
Abraham and Isaac walked, the God who has been my shepherd all my life to this day,

112
00:16:11,129 --> 00:16:16,209
[Reader] the angel who has redeemed me from all harm—may He bless these boys.

113
00:16:16,259 --> 00:16:25,613
[Reader] And may they be called by my name and the names of my fathers Abraham and
Isaac, and may they grow into a multitude upon the earth.”

114
00:16:25,663 --> 00:16:36,370
[Reader] When Joseph saw that his father had placed his right hand on Ephraim’s
head, he was displeased and took his father’s hand to move it from Ephraim’s head to
Manasseh’s.

115
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:48,036
[Reader] “Not so, my father!” Joseph said. “This one is the firstborn; put your
right hand on his head.” But his father refused. “I know, my son, I know!” he said.

116
00:16:48,086 --> 00:16:55,835
[Reader] “He too shall become a people, and he too shall be great; nevertheless, his
younger brother shall be greater than he,

117
00:16:55,885 --> 00:16:59,501
[Reader] and his offspring shall become a multitude of nations.”

118
00:16:59,551 --> 00:17:09,569
[Reader] So that day Jacob blessed them and said: “By you shall Israel pronounce
this blessing: ‘May God make you like Ephraim and Manasseh.’”

119
00:17:09,619 --> 00:17:11,700
[Reader] So he put Ephraim before Manasseh.

120
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:22,362
Count what Joseph did, because Joseph is the sighted man in the room and he did
everything a sighted man does.

121
00:17:22,412 --> 00:17:33,614
He placed the two by eye, the older to his father's right hand, the younger to his
left, so that a blind man reaching straight out could not get it wrong.

122
00:17:33,664 --> 00:17:45,011
He watched the hands. When he saw them cross, he reached in and took hold of his
father's hand to put it where it belonged. Every one of those is kindness.

123
00:17:45,061 --> 00:17:54,618
Every one of those is also the assumption that the man in the bed did not know what
he was doing. And now count what Jacob did.

124
00:17:54,668 --> 00:18:06,008
He knew which man was under which hand, and nobody had told him, and his hands could
not have told him either: two grown men, a couple of years apart.

125
00:18:06,058 --> 00:18:14,767
He knew the custom: a father sets the firstborn to the right. He knew his son, and
that his son would follow it.

126
00:18:14,817 --> 00:18:21,141
And he knew, before he reached out, which one God meant to be greater.

127
00:18:21,191 --> 00:18:33,329
He crossed his arms on purpose, and the Hebrew word for what he did with his hands
means to act with understanding, the way the old translation has it,

128
00:18:33,379 --> 00:18:35,589
guiding his hands wittingly.

129
00:18:35,639 --> 00:18:45,752
And when the hand came in to correct him, he did not let it. Notice how verse 17
begins: when Joseph saw.

130
00:18:45,802 --> 00:18:55,550
In the whole chapter that is the last time anyone trusts his eyes, and it is the
sighted man, and he is the one who is wrong.

131
00:18:56,600 --> 00:19:05,430
Now imagine that one moment, the one the text gives in half a verse. Joseph took his
father's hand to move it.

132
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:19,862
Jacob's hands are on two heads. He knows which is which, because Joseph set them the
way every father does, firstborn to the right, and he knows his son.

133
00:19:19,912 --> 00:19:32,184
His hands could not tell him. He did not need them to. The blessing is half said.
And then he feels a grip on his right wrist. Not a boy's hand.

134
00:19:32,234 --> 00:19:45,331
A man's, strong, careful, a hand that has signed for the grain of Egypt. He knows
that hand. He has felt it on him since it was a boy's hand.

135
00:19:45,381 --> 00:19:53,630
It is lifting his hand off the smaller head, gently, the way you move an old man's
hand off a thing he has got wrong.

136
00:19:55,180 --> 00:20:01,290
[Joseph] Not so, my father. This one is the firstborn. Put your right hand on his
head.

137
00:20:02,026 --> 00:20:03,750
[Israel] I know, my son. I know.

138
00:20:06,300 --> 00:20:17,045
I know. Seventy years earlier, in another room, a sighted son's hand went out to a
blind father's and the blind father was fooled by it.

139
00:20:17,095 --> 00:20:27,516
This time the hand goes out to correct, not to deceive, and the blind father is not
moved by it. He had not made a mistake.

140
00:20:27,566 --> 00:20:36,755
He had made a choice, and he had made it with the only thing anyone has ever been
able to make that choice with, which is not eyes.

141
00:20:36,805 --> 00:20:47,133
Isaac blessed the wrong son by accident and let it stand. Jacob blessed the younger
son on purpose and would not be talked out of it.

142
00:20:47,183 --> 00:20:58,550
The same family, the same hands, the same crossing of the older and the younger, and
the second blind man is the one who gets it right.

143
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:09,915
That is where the two stories part. Isaac used his hands to find out who was there,
and they were lied to. Jacob did not use his hands to find out anything.

144
00:21:09,965 --> 00:21:20,616
He knew before he reached out, and his hands did what he knew. Isaac's hands were
his eyes. Jacob's hands were his will.

145
00:21:20,666 --> 00:21:29,294
And the man behind them had spent a lifetime learning what eyes are worth. At the
wagons he still had to see to believe.

146
00:21:29,344 --> 00:21:38,650
Here he cannot see, and he believes anyway, and acts on it, and will not be moved.

147
00:21:38,700 --> 00:21:40,150
Brian, what do you hear in this one?

148
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:47,616
[Brian] Joseph reached in to move his father's hand because he assumed that a man
who could not see did not know what he was doing.

149
00:21:47,666 --> 00:21:57,090
[Brian] Jacob's answer was two words, said twice: I know. The Hebrew verb there is
yada, and the old dictionary says it means to know, properly to ascertain by seeing.

150
00:21:57,140 --> 00:22:03,209
[Brian] Genesis gives the seeing-verb for knowing to a man who could hardly see, and
it gives it to him about something no eyes and no hands could have told him:

151
00:22:03,259 --> 00:22:05,089
[Brian] which of two grown men God meant to be greater.

152
00:22:05,139 --> 00:22:10,753
[Brian] He had been told, and he knew it the way you know a thing you have seen.
That is the whole page for me.

153
00:22:10,803 --> 00:22:20,762
[Brian] Blind people are underestimated because sighted people tend to think sight
is a vastly superior sense to all the others. It is not.

154
00:22:20,812 --> 00:22:27,582
[Brian] We move through this world by a combination of senses, and most of what we
know does not come in through the eyes anyway.

155
00:22:27,632 --> 00:22:34,682
[Brian] When a fire truck comes along, you hear it long before you see it, and the
hearing is what tells you to pull over.

156
00:22:34,732 --> 00:22:42,826
[Brian] Our senses work together to get information to a brain that is locked away
from the world behind a thick skull, and what that brain does with it, remembering,
judging,

157
00:22:42,876 --> 00:22:44,387
[Brian] believing, needs no eyes at all.

158
00:22:44,437 --> 00:22:49,443
[Brian] Jacob's eyes had gone. His memory and his judgment had not, and neither had
his faith.

159
00:22:49,493 --> 00:22:57,206
[Brian] He knew where his son had set them, crossed his arms on purpose, and when
the sighted man in the room tried to correct him,

160
00:22:57,256 --> 00:23:01,560
[Brian] he said what every blind person has had to say at some point: I know.

161
00:23:01,610 --> 00:23:04,171
[Brian] Loss of sight does not take understanding with it.

162
00:23:04,221 --> 00:23:11,497
[Brian] It does not take faith, or judgment, or memory, or kindness, or any of the
many things that make a person who they are.

163
00:23:11,547 --> 00:23:22,722
[Brian] The writer of Hebrews picked this scene, out of all of Jacob's long life, as
his one act of faith: by faith Jacob, when he was dying, blessed each of Joseph's
sons.

164
00:23:22,772 --> 00:23:33,078
[Brian] Dying, nearly blind, and that is the moment chosen. One more thing I
noticed. Genesis does not use the same word for Isaac's sight loss and for Jacob's.

165
00:23:33,128 --> 00:23:42,972
[Brian] Isaac's eyes were kahah, weak, grown dim. Jacob's were kabed, heavy, which
is the Bible's word for a burden. I know both of those.

166
00:23:43,022 --> 00:23:49,957
[Brian] Some days sight loss is a dimming you learn to live around. Some days it is
a weight you carry.

167
00:23:50,007 --> 00:23:57,423
[Brian] Jacob carried his to the end of the chapter and blessed twelve sons by name
with it on him. And one more.

168
00:23:57,473 --> 00:24:03,428
[Brian] Jacob did not see clearly for most of his life, and he saw clearly at the
end.

169
00:24:03,478 --> 00:24:11,819
[Brian] His eyes fooled him about the robe, the dark fooled him about his wife, and
at the wagons he still would not believe good news until he had seen it.

170
00:24:11,869 --> 00:24:21,326
[Brian] Then, when his eyes could hardly see at all, he put his hands on two grown
grandsons and knew which was which, and would not be corrected.

171
00:24:21,376 --> 00:24:30,050
[Brian] The one time his sight was gone was the one time he got it right. You cannot
always trust your eyes. Jacob learned that the hard way, and so did I.

172
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:42,832
Now the old books. Matthew Henry noticed the same two things Brian did, that Jacob
went blind the way his father did, and that the word is a heavy one.

173
00:24:42,882 --> 00:24:44,150
He put them in one breath.

174
00:24:44,700 --> 00:24:50,196
[Matthew Henry] Those that have the honour of age must therewith be content to take
the burden of it.

175
00:24:50,246 --> 00:24:55,050
[Matthew Henry] The eye of faith may be very clear even when the eye of the body is
very much clouded.

176
00:24:56,300 --> 00:25:07,217
Brian put that second sentence on his page, because it is what the story is about.
Henry goes on to the crossed hands, and he is careful about what they were not.

177
00:25:07,267 --> 00:25:18,397
Jacob did not cross them by mistake, he says, nor in a humour, nor from a partial
affection to one more than the other, but from a spirit of prophecy,

178
00:25:18,447 --> 00:25:21,304
in compliance with the divine counsels.

179
00:25:21,354 --> 00:25:26,928
Then he makes a list, and it is worth hearing, because Jacob is on it twice.

180
00:25:26,978 --> 00:25:39,844
God, he says, has so often advanced the younger above the elder: Abel above Cain,
Isaac above Ishmael, Jacob above Esau, Ephraim above Manasseh,

181
00:25:39,894 --> 00:25:42,625
David above his elder brothers.

182
00:25:42,675 --> 00:25:54,505
He tied the Jews to observe the birthright, Henry says, but he never tied himself to
observe it. Which means the crossed hands were not a blind man's confusion.

183
00:25:54,555 --> 00:26:01,650
They were the family pattern, and the one man in the room who could not see was the
one who could see it.

184
00:26:02,962 --> 00:26:18,549
Now the word. Each of these six stories has its own word for eyes that fail, and
they make a chain. Zedekiah's word was "ah-VAR," to blind, a thing done to a man.

185
00:26:18,599 --> 00:26:35,659
Isaac's was "kah-HAH," to grow dim. Jacob's is a different word again: "kah-VED."
Heavy. Verse 10 says, in the Hebrew, that Israel's eyes were heavy from age.

186
00:26:35,709 --> 00:26:46,387
It is one of the commonest words in the Bible, and it is almost never about eyes. It
is the word for Pharaoh's heart when he hardened it, heavy.

187
00:26:46,437 --> 00:27:00,555
It is the word for the people's ears in Isaiah when they would not hear, heavy. And
with a slightly different vowel it is kavod, glory, the weight of God's presence.

188
00:27:00,605 --> 00:27:09,978
The same root carries the hardest hearts and the glory of God, and once, here, an
old man's eyes.

189
00:27:10,028 --> 00:27:20,497
So the chain so far runs: a king's eyes put out, a father's eyes grown dim, a son's
eyes grown heavy.

190
00:27:20,547 --> 00:27:29,081
Two of those happened the ordinary way, by age, to two men in one family, and
Genesis gave each of them his own word.

191
00:27:29,131 --> 00:27:36,336
Brian hears the difference between them, a dimming and a weight, and the Bible seems
to as well.

192
00:27:36,386 --> 00:27:45,550
Next time the chain goes back to Isaac's word, dim, and adds one more, for an old
priest whose eyes had stopped moving.

193
00:27:46,934 --> 00:27:51,159
What came after. Jacob was not finished.

194
00:27:51,209 --> 00:28:01,850
The next chapter opens with him calling all twelve of his sons to the bed, and it
ends with a sentence that is easy to read past. Genesis chapter 49, verse 28.

195
00:28:02,100 --> 00:28:13,050
[Reader] These are the tribes of Israel, twelve in all, and this was what their
father said to them. He blessed them, and he blessed each one with a suitable
blessing.

196
00:28:13,800 --> 00:28:26,409
Each one with a suitable blessing. Twelve men, in order, by name, with a word fitted
to each, from a man who could not see which of them was standing where.

197
00:28:26,459 --> 00:28:32,603
He would have known them by their voices and their places, the way Isaac should have
known Jacob.

198
00:28:32,653 --> 00:28:37,250
Then he gave the instructions about the cave one more time, and verse 33.

199
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:47,050
[Reader] When Jacob had finished instructing his sons, he pulled his feet into the
bed and breathed his last, and he was gathered to his people.

200
00:28:47,600 --> 00:29:00,105
His sons did as he had charged them. They carried him up out of Egypt, two weeks'
walk at two o'clock, and buried him in the cave at Machpelah with Abraham and Isaac.

201
00:29:00,155 --> 00:29:07,724
The blind father and the blind son, in one cave. Joseph could not follow them.

202
00:29:07,774 --> 00:29:18,742
He was bound to Egypt by his office, and he died there at a hundred and ten, having
made the sons of Israel swear to carry his bones up when God brought them out.

203
00:29:18,792 --> 00:29:29,366
Four hundred years later they did, and Joshua buried him at Shechem, on the ridge of
land Jacob had handed him at the end of this chapter,

204
00:29:29,416 --> 00:29:31,753
one portion above his brothers.

205
00:29:31,803 --> 00:29:40,934
The adoption of his sons and the oath about his bones were two halves of one thing:
a man who could not leave,

206
00:29:40,984 --> 00:29:46,192
securing his place in the promise through the people who could.

207
00:29:46,242 --> 00:29:55,864
And the New Testament, in the chapter that lists the people who lived by faith,
gives the father and the son two verses, back to back.

208
00:29:55,914 --> 00:29:59,750
Hebrews chapter 11, verses 20 and 21.

209
00:30:00,300 --> 00:30:05,810
[Reader] By faith Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau concerning the future.

210
00:30:05,860 --> 00:30:13,750
[Reader] By faith Jacob, when he was dying, blessed each of Joseph’s sons and
worshiped as he leaned on the top of his staff.

211
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:25,957
Two blind men, two blessings, and the Bible's one word for both of them is faith.
Isaac's came by faith after his hands had failed him.

212
00:30:26,007 --> 00:30:36,881
Jacob's came by faith with his hands doing exactly what he meant them to. And
leaning on the top of his staff, which is the one detail Hebrews adds.

213
00:30:36,931 --> 00:30:42,450
He was holding on to something while he did it. Blind men usually are.

214
00:30:43,500 --> 00:30:45,250
Brian, with the last word.

215
00:30:46,197 --> 00:30:53,736
[Brian] Every blind person I know has had a hand reach in to move theirs. Usually it
is kind. Usually the person is sure they are helping.

216
00:30:53,786 --> 00:31:00,698
[Brian] And the hardest two words to say in that moment, without bristle, are the
ones Jacob said. I know. He did not argue.

217
00:31:00,748 --> 00:31:03,839
[Brian] He said I know, said why in one sentence, and went on with the blessing.

218
00:31:03,889 --> 00:31:12,258
[Brian] He had learned in a tent in Beersheba what hands can be made to say, and he
spent the rest of his life finding out that eyes are no better.

219
00:31:12,308 --> 00:31:22,078
[Brian] A lifetime learning that the senses cannot always be trusted, but that what
the Lord has said can. That is what he was leaning on.

220
00:31:22,128 --> 00:31:23,710
[Brian] It is there for all of us to lean on too.

221
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:34,593
Next time, the second pair ends and the third begins: two old men of Shiloh.

222
00:31:34,643 --> 00:31:39,191
A house of God at night, a lamp that has not yet gone out,

223
00:31:39,241 --> 00:31:48,150
and a boy who keeps running to the wrong bedside because the voice that is calling
him is one he has never heard.

224
00:31:48,200 --> 00:31:57,650
The old priest in the bed cannot see the lamp. He is the one who knows whose voice
it is. Eli, and the voice in the night.

225
00:32:00,160 --> 00:32:09,476
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

226
00:32:09,526 --> 00:32:20,290
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

227
00:32:20,340 --> 00:32:26,350
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

